<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 08: Integrating Equations of Motion in 1D</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 5.1 through 5.3.1
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
             You might be worried about the mathematical argument in 5.2 being unsound, so let's show it in
             a different way. Start with the quantity <m>K=\frac{1}{2} m\dot{x}^2</m> and show that 
             <m>\frac{dK}{dt}=F\dot{x} </m>. Show how this implies that
             <m>\Delta K=\int F(x)dx</m> (you'll need the chain rule). 
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A force of constant magnitude <m>F</m> acts on a particle of mass <m>m</m> that can only move
                in one dimension. Determine the sign of the work done by the force on the particle for the situations
                listed below. Your answer can just be <q>positive</q> or <q>negative</q> for each question.

                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The force points to the right and the particle moves to the right.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The force points to the right and the particle moves to the left.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The force points to the left and the particle moves to the right.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The force points to the left and the particle moves to the left.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Example 5.1 solves for the max height. Instead of doing that, solve for the speed of the particle 
            <m>v</m> as a function of <m>z</m>. If we want to know the particle's velocity, what do we have to take 
            into account? Why are there two acceptable solutions for the velocity?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Use your answer from the previous question to explain why technically the expression for 
            <m>\frac{dx}{dt}</m> in example 5.2 is not quite correct, but nevertheless the solution 
            <m>x(t)=x_0 \cos⁡(\omega t)</m> doesn't suffer from the same issue.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                 Take the expression for the escape velocity from example 5.3 and find its value in terms of the 
                 mass <m>M</m> and radius <m>R</m> of a spherical body, then find the value of the radius 
                 <m>R_s</m>, given a mass, for the escape velocity to be the speed of light. This is called the 
                 Schwarzschild radius: the size an object of mass <m>M</m> would have to be to become a black hole.
                 Calculate this value for the Earth.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>